= Turmeric

_Ayurvedic status: fully classical — haridrā / hariḍ is one of the most-used drugs of the saṃhitās, with multiple distinct named forms. Chinese: fully classical — 薑黃 (jiānghuáng) and the related 鬱金 (yùjīn) are canonical Bencao drugs with the important warning that the two are botanically distinct organs of related Curcuma species and are pharmacologically different. Greco-Roman: marginal — late Latin /_ curcuma _via Arabic; the substance was known to medieval Arabic pharmacy and reached Europe through that route, not directly through Greco-Roman tradition._

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*Latin:* _Curcuma longa_ L., family Zingiberaceae (the ginger family). The drug is the *rhizome*, dried and processed. The related _C. aromatica_ Salisb. (Indian "wild turmeric," _vana-haridrā_) and _C. zedoaria_ Roscoe (zedoary, _karcūra_) are distinct species, and the closely-related _C. xanthorrhiza_ (Java turmeric) and _C. amada_ (mango ginger) are also separate drugs. _Curcuma_ is a late-Latin Linnaean adaptation of the Arabic / Persian _kurkum_ (كركم) — itself probably from the same Semitic root as Hebrew _karkōm_ (כַּרְכֹּם, "crocus" — applied to saffron and to turmeric as the two yellow-dyeing drugs, blurring of which has caused intermittent confusion across two millennia). _longa_ = "long" (of the rhizome).#footnote[Etymology: standard botanical eponymic literature; the Arabic _kurkum_ etymology is widely accepted, with the Hebrew _karkōm_ (saffron) cognate noted in Liddell-Scott's _Greek-English Lexicon_ on κρόκος (_krókos_). The saffron / turmeric naming-overlap is a real source of cross-text identification difficulty in pre-modern materia medica.]

*English:* _Turmeric_, from Middle English _turmeryte_, from Anglo-Norman / Old French _terre-merite_ ("worthy earth" — referring to the yellow earth-pigment), from Medieval Latin _terra merita_. The folk etymology is well-attested and the OED accepts it.#footnote[_Oxford English Dictionary_ (s.v. "turmeric, n."); the _terra merita_ etymology is standard.]

*Chinese:* The classical two-drug distinction:
- *薑黃 (jiānghuáng)*, "ginger-yellow" — the *main rhizome* of Curcuma species (_C. longa_ proper, also _C. aromatica_, _C. wenyujin_). This is *turmeric proper* in the Chinese sense — yellow inside, the canonical seasoning and dye.
- *鬱金 (yùjīn)*, "constraint-gold" — the *lateral tuberous roots* of various Curcuma species (especially _C. aromatica_, _C. wenyujin_, _C. kwangsiensis_, and _C. longa_). Pharmacologically distinct from 薑黃, with different actions (Liver-channel, cooling, blood-moving).
- *莪朮 (ézhú)*, zedoary — the rhizome of _C. zedoaria_ and related species; another distinct drug.
The 薑黃 / 鬱金 distinction — *same plant, different organ* — is one of the foundational drug-organ differentiations of Chinese pharmacy and is a frequent source of mis-identification in the popular literature.#footnote[薑黃 / 鬱金 / 莪朮 distinction: _Bencao Gangmu_ (本草綱目), 1578, grass section; the species mapping is botanically complex and varies by region — Bensky, Clavey & Stöger, _Chinese Herbal Medicine: Materia Medica_, 3rd ed. (Eastland Press, 2004), treats them in separate entries with full species notes. The confusion of 薑黃 with 鬱金 in modern translations of TCM is one of the standard error patterns.]

*Sanskrit:* हरिद्रा (_haridrā_) — the principal Sanskrit name; etymology from _hari-_ ("green/yellow/tawny — the color of gold and of dawn"; _hari_ is also an epithet of Viṣṇu and of the lion, all sharing the yellow-tawny semantic field) + the formative -दra. Other names: रजनी (_rajanī_, "the night" — possibly referring to the dark dried rhizome contrasted with the yellow interior), निशा (_niśā_, "night" — same), वरवर्णिनी (_varavarṇinī_, "the excellent-complexioned"), कांचनी (_kāñcanī_, "the golden one"), यशस्विनी (_yaśasvinī_, "the famous one"). The fresh / dried distinction is less developed than for ginger but is recognized; _vana-haridrā_ ("forest turmeric") = _Curcuma aromatica_; _dāru-haridrā_ ("wood turmeric") = the unrelated _Berberis aristata_, a Berberidaceae drug, *not* a turmeric — important confusion to flag.#footnote[Monier-Williams, _A Sanskrit–English Dictionary_ (s.v. _haridrā_, _hari_, _rajanī_, _niśā_, _kāñcanī_); synonym lists from Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Haritakyādi-varga_, _haridrā_ entry. The _dāru-haridrā_ = _Berberis aristata_ (the major South Asian berberine drug) is a critical disambiguation: same modifier-noun construction, completely different plant family and chemistry.]

== Classical Chinese Medicine (中醫) View

薑黃 is a fully canonical drug, although less central than ginger; it sits in a specific role and is best understood in contradistinction to 鬱金.

*薑黃 (jiānghuáng, turmeric rhizome):*
- Property: *warm* (溫); Flavor: pungent (辛), bitter (苦); Channels: Spleen (脾), Liver (肝).
- Actions: 破血行氣 — *breaks blood and moves qi* (the principal action — for blood stasis with cold pattern); 通經止痛 — opens the channels and stops pain (chest/abdominal pain, dysmenorrhea from blood stasis); 利膽 — promotes bile flow; topically for swelling, sores.
- *Cautions:* contraindicated in pregnancy; cautious in heat-pattern blood stasis (the warming character is not suitable).#footnote[薑黃 entry: _Bencao Gangmu_ 薑黃 section; Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed.]

*鬱金 (yùjīn, the lateral tubers — comparative note):*
- Property: *cool* (涼); Flavor: pungent, bitter; Channels: Liver (肝), Heart (心), Lung (肺).
- Actions: 活血行氣 — invigorates blood and moves qi (cooler register than 薑黃); 清心解鬱 — clears the Heart and resolves constraint (a *psychiatric / emotional* indication); 利膽退黃 — promotes bile flow, alleviates jaundice (a Liver-clearing indication); 涼血止血 — cools the blood and stops bleeding from heat.
- *Cross-contrast:* whereas 薑黃 *breaks* blood with warming, 鬱金 *invigorates* blood with cooling and reaches the Heart and Lung. The Bencao tradition treats them as different drugs precisely because they answer different patterns.#footnote[鬱金 entry: _Bencao Gangmu_; Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed. The 薑黃 / 鬱金 functional differentiation is one of the classic Chinese-pharmacy organ-by-organ same-plant distinctions.]

== Ayurvedic Dravyaguna (द्रव्यगुण)

Haridrā is one of the most-used Ayurvedic drugs, with applications spanning internal medicine, dermatology, gynaecology, ENT, and ritual practice.#footnote[Haridrā in Caraka Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 4 (_lekhanīya gaṇa_, "scraping" group; _kuṣṭha-ghna gaṇa_, anti-skin-disease group; _viṣa-ghna gaṇa_, anti-poison group; among several other gaṇa memberships); full treatment in Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Haritakyādi-varga_; Suśruta Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 38. Translations: Sharma & Dash, _Caraka_ (Chowkhamba); Murthy, _Bhāvaprakāśa_ (Chaukhamba).]

*Rasa (taste):* Tikta (तिक्त, bitter), Kaṭu (कटु, pungent).

*Guṇa (qualities):* Laghu (लघु, light), Rūkṣa (रूक्ष, dry).

*Vīrya (potency):* Uṣṇa (उष्ण, warming).

*Vipāka (post-digestive taste):* Kaṭu (कटु, pungent).#footnote[Rasa–guṇa–vīrya–vipāka of haridrā: Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Haritakyādi-varga_; standard dravyaguna. Note that haridrā shares the bitter-pungent / light-dry / warming / pungent-vipāka profile with most _kuṣṭha-ghna_ ("anti-skin-disease") drugs.]

*Prabhāva (specific action):* The classical commentary highlights haridrā's near-universal anti-_kuṣṭha_ (skin-disease) and _viṣa-ghna_ (anti-poison) action, and its specific application as a *blood-purifier* (_rakta-śodhana_) and *uterine-cleansing* (_garbhāśaya-śodhana_) agent, as *prabhāva-level* — actions not fully derivable from the standard parameters. The Caraka inclusion in *both* the _lekhanīya_ ("scraping" — i.e. removing accumulated tissue / cleansing) and the _viṣa-ghna_ groups is itself a marker of an unusual cross-functional drug. Flagged as commentarial/interpretive.#footnote[On prabhāva: Caraka Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 26.67–72; on the gaṇa memberships (lekhanīya, kuṣṭha-ghna, viṣa-ghna), Caraka Sūtrasthāna 4. The prabhāva-level reading is the standard modern dravyaguna position (cf. P. V. Sharma, _Dravyaguṇa-vijñāna_, vol. II) and is interpretive.]

*Doṣa effect:* Pacifies Kapha and Vāta (the laghu-rūkṣa-uṣṇa profile); slightly Pitta-aggravating with excess use but generally well-tolerated due to its bitter-cooling rasa-counterbalance.#footnote[Doṣa attribution: Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Haritakyādi-varga_.]

*Karma (actions):*
- _Kuṣṭha-ghna_ — anti-skin-disease (the principal classical application; for _kuṣṭha_ in its broad classical sense of obstinate skin disease).
- _Viṣa-ghna_ — anti-poison; classical use in poisoning, infections, and (modern restatement) inflammatory states.
- _Rakta-śodhana_ — blood-purifying (the classical category encompassing skin-disease, gout, chronic inflammation).
- _Krimi-ghna_ — antimicrobial (in the classical sense).
- _Vraṇa-ropaṇa_ — wound-healing (topical and internal).
- _Varṇya_ — complexion-enhancing (the canonical Indian use, with haridrā paste applied to the skin before weddings — the cosmetic-medical convergence).
- _Pramehaghna_ — for _prameha_ (urinary disorders / diabetic-spectrum).
- _Garbhāśaya-śodhana_, _ārtava-janana_ — uterine cleansing, promotes menstruation; classical use in menstrual irregularity and post-partum recovery.
- _Kāsa-śvāsa-hara_ — relieves cough and dyspnea (Kapha-clearing).
- _Yakṛt-uttejaka_ (modern terminology) — Liver-stimulating, choleretic.
- Used in: _kuṣṭha_, _prameha_, _pāṇḍu_ (anemia), _rakta-pitta_ (mildly), _kṛmi-roga_, _kaṇḍū_ (itching), chronic respiratory disease, _yoni-vyāpat_ (gynaecological disorders), _viṣa_ (poisoning, infection), and as a daily _rakta-śodhana_ / preventive food-medicine.#footnote[Karma compiled from Caraka Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 4 (gaṇa memberships) and Cikitsāsthāna chapters on _kuṣṭha_ (7), _prameha_ (6), _kṛmi-roga_ (20); Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Haritakyādi-varga_.]

=== Viruddha (विरुद्ध — incompatibilities)

_(tagged by source tier — S = core saṃhitā, N = later nighaṇṭu, F = folk/regional, M = modern/pharmacological)_

- *(S/N) Conditional cautions:* Pitta excess, _rakta-pitta_ (bleeding from heat), high fever with Pitta, pregnancy in heavy doses (uterine-stimulating; classical caution); gastritis with heat pattern.
- *(N) Saṃyoga viruddha:* heavy concurrent use with strong-warming pungent foods may aggravate Pitta.
- *(M) Modern / pharmacological:*
  - *Anticoagulant / antiplatelet interaction:* curcumin and related curcuminoids inhibit platelet aggregation and have anticoagulant activity; case reports of INR elevation with warfarin + turmeric supplements. Use cautiously with warfarin, NOACs, aspirin, clopidogrel.
  - *Gallstones / biliary obstruction:* turmeric stimulates bile flow; symptomatic cholelithiasis is a relative contraindication (potential biliary colic).
  - *Pregnancy:* dietary use is generally considered safe; *high-dose supplements (>1 g curcumin/day)* are not recommended in pregnancy due to the classical uterine-stimulating concern and limited modern safety data.
  - *Hepatotoxicity:* documented case reports of hepatitis with turmeric / curcumin supplements (especially products enhanced with piperine or formulated for high bioavailability), in some cases attributable to adulteration (Pb-chromate yellow coloring in cheap turmeric — a serious sourcing concern). The case literature has grown; *flag rare but real hepatotoxicity.*
  - *Iron absorption:* turmeric may modestly inhibit iron absorption (high doses); relevant in iron-deficiency anaemia management.
  - *Chemotherapy interaction:* theoretical interference with platinum-class chemotherapy via the antioxidant effect; clinically relevant in oncology contexts.
  - *Curcumin bioavailability is intrinsically low* — much of the popular literature on curcumin's clinical effects involves bioavailability-enhanced formulations (piperine-, phospholipid-, micellar-formulated curcuminoids), and the raw-spice clinical effect is more modest than the supplement-trial data suggest.#footnote[Anticoagulant interaction and hepatotoxicity case reports: Mills & Bone, _Herb Contraindications and Drug Interactions_; recent reviews in _Liver International_, _Drug Safety_, _Journal of Hepatology_. The lead-chromate adulteration is a known issue in some commercial low-grade turmeric powders (chiefly Bangladesh sourcing — documented Bangladesh-FAO surveys, ~2010s) — a sourcing-quality issue. Curcumin bioavailability: well-established pharmacology — verify against current reviews in _AAPS Journal_ and _Molecular Nutrition & Food Research_.]

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

_Turmeric enters European-Mediterranean medicine via Tibb — specifically through Avicenna's treatment of_ kurkum _and the Arabic medieval transmission. See_ tibb-al-arabi.typ.

*Arabic:* كركم (_kurkum_) — same Semitic-Persian root that gives the genus Curcuma; هرد (_hurd_) in some regional usage. Persian: زردچوبه (_zardchūbe_, "yellow wood"). Urdu/Hakeemi: ہلدی (_haldī_, Sanskrit-derived); زردچوبہ (_zardchūbah_); کرکم (_kurkum_).

*Mizāj:* *Hot and Dry in the 2nd–3rd degree* (Ibn Sīnā, *al-Qānūn* II, _kurkum_). The Hot-Dry classification converges with the Ayurvedic _uṣṇa vīrya_ and the Chinese 溫 — tri-system convergence.

*Af'āl:* *Mufattiḥ li-l-suddad* (deobstruent for hepatic and splenic obstruction); *muḥallil* (resolvent); *muḥsin li-l-lawn* (complexion-improving, the classical cosmetic register); *muḥarrik al-bāh* (mild aphrodisiac); *muqawwī al-mi'da*; *jālī* (cleansing, internal and topical); *muqawwī al-baṣar* (eye-strengthening); *mukhrij li-l-balgham* (phlegm-expectorant); *muqaṭṭiʿ* (cuts viscous humors); *muḥsin li-l-bashara* (skin-clarifying — the classical cosmetic application paralleling the Ayurvedic _varṇya_).

*Istemālāt:* hepatic and splenic obstruction; jaundice; chronic skin disease; chronic phlegmatic conditions; cold-pattern dyspepsia; cosmetic skin-clearing preparations; topical for wounds and inflammation; pulmonary chronic conditions.

*Mawāne':* hot-pattern conditions in heavy dose; bleeding disorders; pregnancy at therapeutic dose; gallstones (modern caution); active peptic ulcer.

*Muṣliḥ:* milk; honey; sugar.

*Badal:* zedoary (*jadwār*); saffron in some indications.

*Tibb formulas:* *Sufūf-i Kurkum* — turmeric powder; *Maʿjūn-i Kurkum*; topical *Marham-i Kurkum* for skin disease; the Mughal-Hakeemi *Itrīfal-i Aftīmūn* and similar triphala-based compounds use turmeric as one of the modifying ingredients; *Yūnānī cosmetic ubṭan* preparations (the South Asian Unani-cosmetic facial-paste tradition, paralleling the Ayurvedic _lepa_ and _ubṭan_ usage) use turmeric centrally.

*Source attestation:* Ibn Sīnā, *al-Qānūn* II, _kurkum_; Ibn al-Bayṭār, *al-Jāmiʿ*; *Khazāyin al-Adwiya*, vol. III, _haldī / kurkum_ entry.#footnote[Tibb kurkum: Avicenna's Canon is the principal European medieval entry point for turmeric, displacing the absent Greco-Roman attestation. Marco Polo's "saffron-substitute" report from China (13th c.) reinforces the medieval European reception via Avicennan plus eyewitness channels.]

== Classical Formulas & Preparations

=== Chinese medicine (中醫)

- *薑黃 single-herb decoction* — 3–10 g; for cold-pattern abdominal/chest pain from blood stasis.
- *烏金散 (Wū Jīn Sǎn)* — 鬱金-led formula for emotional constraint with breast distension.
- *白金丸 (Bái Jīn Wán)* — 鬱金 + 白礬 (alum); classical formula for phlegm-misting the Heart with mania / psychiatric pattern.
- *膏摩 (gāomó) and topical preparations* — turmeric used in plaster preparations for trauma, contusion, swelling.#footnote[Formula sources: _Bencao Gangmu_; Bensky & Barolet, _Formulas & Strategies_, 2nd ed. 白金丸 is a classical 涤痰 (phlegm-clearing) formula.]

=== Ayurveda

- *Haridrā cūrṇa* — the simple powder, ~1–3 g taken with warm milk (the famous "golden milk" — _haridrā-kṣīra_), water, or honey; the foundational classical preparation.
- *Haridrā-khaṇḍa (हरिद्राखण्ड)* — a classical compound preparation: turmeric in a sugar-and-spice _khaṇḍa_ (granulated medicated sugar) base; for chronic urticaria, eczema, allergic skin disease (a top classical formula for chronic skin allergies; ~5–10 g taken with milk).
- *Mahāmañjiṣṭhādi kvātha* — blood-purifying decoction with haridrā among the major ingredients; for chronic skin disease.
- *Nimbādi cūrṇa, Pañca-tikta ghṛta* — bitter-tonic and ghee preparations with haridrā as ingredient.
- *Khadirāriṣṭa* — fermented preparation with haridrā for chronic skin disease.
- *Lepa preparations* — topical pastes with haridrā for skin disease, wounds, and cosmetic use (the canonical pre-wedding _haldi_ ceremony of South Asian cultures applies haridrā paste to the bride and groom — a folk-ritual continuation of the _varṇya_ (complexion) and ritually-purifying classical use).
- *Daśamūla taila variants* — turmeric in compound oils for joint disorders.#footnote[Formula sources: _Bhaiṣajyaratnāvalī_ (Haridrā-khaṇḍa); _Sharangadhara Saṃhitā_; _Ayurvedic Formulary of India_, Part I. The _haldi_ pre-wedding ritual is a folk-religious continuation of the classical _varṇya_ use; the cosmetic and ritual register has been continuous for at least two millennia in South Asia.]

=== Western / Greco-Roman and later European medicine

Turmeric was *not* in the classical Greco-Roman pharmacy in any clear identification — its principal entry into Mediterranean medicine was through *Arabic pharmacy* (Avicenna, _Canon of Medicine_, c. 1025 CE, treats _kurkum_ as a Liver and uterine stimulant), and from there into medieval European pharmacy. The pre-Arabic Mediterranean *might* have known turmeric as one of several yellow-dyeing-and-medicinal substances (the saffron / turmeric confusion), but identifications in Dioscorides, Pliny, and Galen are uncertain.

- *Medieval Arabic / European pharmacy:* turmeric as *kurkum* in Arabic pharmacy; entered European pharmacopoeia through the Latin translations of Avicenna and from Arabic traders. Used for Liver and gall complaints, jaundice (consistent with the classical Chinese 鬱金 indication), and as a dye.
- *Modern Western:* a long quiet period in formal European pharmacy; the late-20th-c. and current revival is driven by *curcumin* research and is the principal modern Western tradition.
- *Marco Polo* (13th c.) recorded turmeric on his return from China as a "vegetable that has all the qualities of saffron with the smell and color similar to it but at much lower cost" — a clean disambiguation of the saffron / turmeric confusion from the European side.#footnote[Avicenna, _al-Qānūn fī-ṭ-ṭibb_, Liber 2 (on simple drugs), entry on _kurkum_; medieval Arabic pharmacy is the substantial Eurasian-medical tradition that brings turmeric into Mediterranean medicine, _not_ Greco-Roman directly. Marco Polo, _Il Milione_, on the lands of Fukien (Fujian). The Greco-Roman identification of turmeric is genuinely uncertain — flagged.]

== Modern Nutrition & Pharmacology

*Key constituents:*
- *Curcuminoids* — the principal active and pigment class: *curcumin* (the major; bright yellow), *demethoxycurcumin*, *bisdemethoxycurcumin*. Together ~2–5% of the dried rhizome.
- *Turmerones* — ar-turmerone, α- and β-turmerone — the aromatic terpene class of the essential oil (~5% of dry rhizome).
- *Polysaccharides* (immunomodulatory fraction).
- *Starch* — substantial.
- *Trace minerals* — iron, manganese.#footnote[Phytochemistry: standard pharmacognosy (Evans, _Trease and Evans' Pharmacognosy_); reviews in _Phytochemistry_, _Journal of Agricultural and Food Chemistry_. Curcumin bioavailability: AAPS / pharmacology literature.]

*Pharmacology of note:*
- *Anti-inflammatory:* curcumin inhibits NF-κB, COX-2, 5-LOX, and several other inflammatory pathways. Substantial preclinical evidence; modest-to-moderate clinical evidence for osteoarthritis pain (reasonable effect), inflammatory bowel disease (supportive), inflammatory skin conditions (the classical _kuṣṭha-ghna_ correlate).
- *Antioxidant:* substantial in vitro; the in vivo clinical translation requires bioavailability-enhanced formulations to be meaningful.
- *Hepatoprotective / choleretic:* increases bile flow; preclinical hepatoprotection against various toxins; clinical evidence modest.
- *Antineoplastic research:* curcumin is a prominent research compound in oncology — broad in vitro and animal-model activity (NF-κB, Wnt, STAT3 inhibition); clinical trials have been disappointing as monotherapy, with bioavailability the chief obstacle.
- *Cardiovascular:* modest improvements in endothelial function, lipid profile.
- *Antimicrobial:* broad-spectrum in vitro; clinical relevance modest.
- *Bioavailability:* curcumin's principal limitation; very low oral bioavailability for the free curcumin form. Piperine (from black pepper) co-administration increases bioavailability ~20× — the modern correlate of the classical Ayurvedic pairing in _trikaṭu_ and many haridrā-containing formulas (turmeric + pepper is a classical compound, not a modern innovation). Phospholipid (Meriva), micellar (NovaSOL), and liposomal formulations achieve much higher bioavailability and are the basis of most positive modern clinical trials.
- *Cross-system convergence:* the classical _kuṣṭha-ghna_ + _rakta-śodhana_ + _yakṛt-uttejaka_ + _viṣa-ghna_ + _vraṇa-ropaṇa_ profile maps closely onto the modern anti-inflammatory + antioxidant + hepatoprotective + antimicrobial + wound-healing pharmacology. The classical pairing with black pepper (piperine) anticipates the bioavailability problem by 2000 years — one of the cleaner classical-modern convergences in herbal pharmacology.#footnote[Anti-inflammatory and clinical evidence: multiple systematic reviews in _Journal of Medicinal Food_, _Phytotherapy Research_, _Foods_; verify against current systematic reviews. Curcumin bioavailability and bioavailability-enhanced formulations: Meriva, NovaSOL, Theracurmin — pharmaceutics literature in _AAPS Journal_ and similar. The piperine / curcumin enhancement is by Shoba et al. (_Planta Medica_, 1998) and subsequent literature.]

*Safe daily dosage / upper limit:*
- *Culinary use:* up to ~3 g of turmeric powder per day in food is broadly considered safe and is the dietary register of South / Southeast Asian cooking.
- *Ayurvedic Pharmacopoeia of India:* 1–3 g/day of powder; 30–60 mL of decoction.
- *Standardized curcumin supplements:* commonly 500–2000 mg/day (often with piperine 5–20 mg/day for bioavailability); clinical-trial ranges 100–6000 mg/day; *flag rare hepatotoxicity especially with high-bioavailability formulations*.
- *Risk groups (use cautiously or avoid high doses):* (a) concurrent *anticoagulant* / antiplatelet therapy; (b) symptomatic *gallstones* / biliary obstruction; (c) *pregnancy* (high-dose supplements); (d) concurrent *chemotherapy* (theoretical antioxidant interference); (e) iron-deficiency anaemia in active replacement; (f) prior turmeric-supplement hepatotoxicity; (g) sourcing — *low-grade commercial turmeric powder may be adulterated with lead-chromate yellow pigment* — a serious sourcing-quality concern; buy from reputable suppliers, prefer whole rhizome or certified-tested powder.
- *Reference figures, not personalized medical advice.*#footnote[Dose ranges and risk groups: Ayurvedic Pharmacopoeia of India, _Haridrā_ monograph; WHO _Monographs on Selected Medicinal Plants_, vol. 1, _Rhizoma Curcumae longae_; current clinical-trial and herb-drug-interaction literature. Lead-chromate adulteration: documented in food-safety surveys; an important sourcing concern.]

